export const body = "<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p>In 1981, <em>Donkey Kong</em> was released, a game in which the player takes control of a carpenter whose girlfriend is held captive by a giant gorilla. On the quest to rescue his darling, the carpenter has to navigate an askew construction site, ascending one platform at a time. (Now you know why these games are called \"platformers.\")</p>\n<p>Of course, the gorilla doesn't make it easy for our protagonist as it continuously unleashes rolling barrels from above to intercept his progress. And to maneuver around the wheeling obstacles, our main character performs a simple yet effective action: he jumps. Thus, he was called \"Jumpman\" in the English instructions of the game.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/e7448e8c11a0438a79fb.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/e7448e8c11a0438a79fb.webp\" width=\"500\" height=\"446\" style=\"--source-width:256px\" alt=\"Illustration 1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/a0947c0c6e8253f1f9f1.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/a0947c0c6e8253f1f9f1.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"575\" style=\"--source-width:290px\" alt=\"Illustration 2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p>Four years after the release of <em>Donkey Kong</em>, Jumpman switched occupation, becoming a plumber who dashed from side to side across the Mushroom Kingdom to, again, rescue a princess. The reason for for this change in profession was the level design in <em>Super Mario Bros.</em> which consisted of many pipes. Only an experienced plumber can navigate in such environments, according to the game's creator.</p>\n<p>In addition to the new career path, this time our player character had an official name, one that both gamers and non-gamers are accustomed to. His name was Mario.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>In the wake of <em>Pac-Man</em>, the developer was seeking a formula for their own best-selling title, and the game’s designer was at the helm of that mission. When he first worked on <em>Donkey Kong</em>, he envisioned a \"maze escape\" game akin to <em>Pac-Man</em>. It was until later when he decided to add a jump command that the game began a new life.</p>\n<p>Initially, the game’s designer wanted to have Popeye the Sailor as the player characte<em>r</em>, but he couldn't acquire the licensing rights, so he promoted his cameo character to the protagonist role instead. The original vision for this unnamed character was to be a virtual embodiment of the game’s designer, who could go on to make cameo appearances in other games from the company. To his surprise, Mario kicked off a new franchise himself and evolved into a gaming icon.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/69dbb8b6f5e5020be635.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/69dbb8b6f5e5020be635.webp\" width=\"640\" height=\"444\" style=\"--source-width:256px\" alt=\"Illustration 3\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td>\n\n</td><td><p>As mentioned before, Mario changes jobs based on the environments he operates in. He was a carpenter in <em>Donkey Kong</em> because the game took place in a construction site; he later became a plumber in <em>Super Mario Bros.</em> because of the pipes and sewers.</p>\n<p>The decision to make Mario a plumber convinced the game’s designer to choose Italy as his home country. Some sources also attribute his mustache to his nationality, but Mario's father has a couple other explanations.</p>\n<p>The first explanation lies in the primitive hardware. The pixelated graphics posed several design problems, mainly concerning the head of the character. Due to the limited pixel count, the game’s designer decided to have Mario wear a cap to avoid rendering his hair, and he added the mustache (on top of a big nose) to help the character's facial features read more clearly.</p>\n<p>The mustache idea was also in part inspired by the landlord whose name was borrowed for Mario. When Mario was being localized for American audiences in 1981, the company got into a heated argument with their warehouse's landlord regarding the back rent. According to the game’s designer, the landlord also had quite a mustache.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>In terms of the color choices, Mario wears a red overalls and a long-sleeved blue shirt to contrast against the black background. If you pay close attention, you will see these two colors reused for many other objects in the game. That was how people optimized their games back in the day.</p>\n<p>We guess the reason why they swapped blue out of Mario's costume in <em>Super Mario Bros</em>. was because of the underwater sections, which were painted in saturated blues. Though these sections were few and far between, they could still dampen the visual clarity of the game had Mario worn a blue shirt.Optimization fun fact</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/ac8f52e089c20a98996f.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/ac8f52e089c20a98996f.webp\" width=\"500\" height=\"298\" style=\"--source-width:240px\" alt=\"Illustration 6\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/14b92f32997cdd20fe3f.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/14b92f32997cdd20fe3f.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"575\" style=\"--source-width:256px\" alt=\"Illustration 7\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/9721a989dc62f37818e9.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/9721a989dc62f37818e9.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"647\" style=\"--source-width:256px\" alt=\"Illustration 8\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p>From a game to a franchise, from a mechanic to a genre, from a cameo to an icon, the history of Mario is indeed one of whimsical fascination. Throughout the years, Mario has changed jobs several times, and as a tradition, he always changed his outfits accordingly. However, we can always tell it is him thanks to the mustache and the nose.</p>\n<p>\"Let's make our main character a portly Italian plumber with a comically large mustache and an equally large nose,\" as a design brief, doesn't inspire a lot of confidence. But once visualized, we can all agree that the final product is beyond charming. So, if your idea sounds ludicrous on paper, don't worry too much. It may turn out brilliant and give birth to a new franchise.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/d3cd699a46b245700af2.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/d3cd699a46b245700af2.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"647\" style=\"--source-width:290px\" alt=\"Illustration 9\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></td></tr></tbody></table></div>";
export const images = ["assets/cookies-inline/14b92f32997cdd20fe3f.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/69dbb8b6f5e5020be635.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/9721a989dc62f37818e9.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/a0947c0c6e8253f1f9f1.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/ac8f52e089c20a98996f.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/d3cd699a46b245700af2.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/e7448e8c11a0438a79fb.webp"];
